# Crypt rooms (DESIGN §3.3). A room is a grid at block resolution (one
# character = 4 × 4 cells), 16 × 10 blocks per slot (a room can be several
# slots wide). `room <name> [room|entrance|goal|secret|ruin]`, then its rows,
# top first; a blank line or the next `room` ends it. Short lines are padded
# with spaces.
#
#   #  wall              .  open (the wall behind it)
#   ,  open to the sky (no wall behind: a broken roof)
#      (space) leave the terrain as it is
#   C  chest: its bottom-left block (a chest is 2 × 2 blocks; the rest '.')
#   T  candle             S  a creature            B  a boss (several)
#   W  water              L  lava                  ^  spikes
#   ?  illusory wall (walk through it)             %  weak wall (one hit)
#   =  planks (solid)     ~  rubble
#   -  a wooden platform: stand on it, jump up through it, hold S to drop
#
# Doors: a side door is rows 1–5 from the bottom of the slot's edge, a hole
# in the top or bottom is columns 6–9. Every room has doors all round (or
# fewer); assembly walls up the ones it doesn't use, and makes one an
# illusory wall to hide a secret room. A room you climb up out of needs
# ledges under its top hole (the player jumps 10 blocks).

